WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.475
Block three.

2
00:00:01.475 --> 00:00:06.025
Convolutional networks on images, from the clinic to the whole cell.

3
00:00:06.025 --> 00:00:14.550
On the page, this block is the sections "What assumptions does a CNN build in?" and "Beyond sequences: a contact map is an image".

4
00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:20.050
In Block two the data was sequence, which is one-dimensional and rather abstract.

5
00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:27.500
Here the data is pictures, which is what convolutional networks were invented for, and where their assumption fits best.

6
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:41.175
We will do four things: finish the architecture story with ResNet, show that more weights are not better, work through what you do when you have almost no labelled data, and then measure the thing convolution does not give you, which is rotation.

7
00:00:41.175 --> 00:00:48.050
And at the end we will go from classifying a tile of tissue to the ambition of modelling an entire human cell.

8
00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:51.425
First, the loose end from Block one.

9
00:00:51.425 --> 00:00:57.550
After AlexNet, people went deeper: VGG with nineteen layers, GoogLeNet with twenty-two.

10
00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:00.300
And then going deeper stopped working.

11
00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:03.325
Now, the obvious guess is overfitting.

12
00:01:03.325 --> 00:01:10.425
A deeper network has more capacity, so perhaps it memorises the training data and does worse on new data.

13
00:01:10.425 --> 00:01:16.425
That is the normal failure, and you have seen it in Session nine and again in Block two of this session.

14
00:01:16.425 --> 00:01:21.300
But that is not what happened, and this is the detail I want you to notice.

15
00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:25.400
The deeper plain networks had a higher error on the training data.

16
00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:27.075
On training.

17
00:01:27.075 --> 00:01:33.775
Overfitting means you do well on training and badly on test; it cannot explain doing badly on training.

18
00:01:33.775 --> 00:01:37.150
So whatever was wrong, it was not overfitting.

19
00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:50.300
The second guess is vanishing gradients: the learning signal, travelling back from the output through many layers, gets multiplied by small numbers at every step until it is effectively zero, and the early layers stop learning.

20
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:55.625
That is a real phenomenon, and it is why batch normalisation was invented.

21
00:01:55.625 --> 00:02:00.650
But He and colleagues checked, by actually measuring the gradients, and they were healthy.

22
00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:02.900
So it was not that either.

23
00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:06.200
What was left is almost philosophically annoying.

24
00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:09.600
The deeper network was simply harder to optimise.

25
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:23.550
And you can prove a good solution exists: take a fifty-six-layer network, copy the weights of a working thirty-four-layer network into its first thirty-four layers, and make the remaining twenty-two layers do nothing, just pass their input through.

26
00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:27.150
That network is exactly as good as the shallower one.

27
00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:31.250
So a solution at least as good must exist in the weight space.

28
00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:34.325
Gradient descent just could not find it.

29
00:02:34.325 --> 00:02:37.450
ResNet's answer is simple to state.

30
00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:46.150
Each block learns a correction to its input, and returns x plus F of x: the input, plus whatever the block decided to add to it.

31
00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:49.275
Look at what that does to the difficult case.

32
00:02:49.275 --> 00:03:00.500
If a block has nothing useful to contribute, it no longer has to learn the identity function, which turns out to be hard; it only has to push F of x towards zero, which is easy.

33
00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:04.825
Doing nothing became the default rather than something to be learned.

34
00:03:04.825 --> 00:03:08.700
And there is a second benefit, which is about the gradient.

35
00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:14.400
The plus-x gives the learning signal a path straight back, skipping the block's own derivatives.

36
00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:20.075
So however deep you stack, there is always a short route from the error back to the early layers.

37
00:03:20.075 --> 00:03:27.050
With a hundred and fifty-two layers, ResNet reached a top-five error of three point six per cent in twenty fifteen.

38
00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:32.625
For comparison, a trained human annotator on that task is around five per cent.

39
00:03:32.625 --> 00:03:40.025
I will add the caveat the page adds: three point six was an ensemble of several networks voting, not a single model.

40
00:03:40.025 --> 00:03:51.950
In the lab, you can see the same effect in miniature: a three-block ResNet with eleven thousand nine hundred and sixty-eight weights beats a three-block VGG network with eight thousand seven hundred and twenty.

41
00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:57.650
The challenge itself ended in twenty seventeen, because the task was considered solved.

42
00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:08.250
But residual connections did not go anywhere: they are in essentially every deep network since, including the Transformers of Session eleven and AlphaFold in Session twelve.

43
00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:14.350
Now the experiment from your own lab, which makes the session's central claim concrete.

44
00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:18.175
Four models, trained on the same BloodMNIST images.

45
00:04:18.175 --> 00:04:26.825
These are photographs of single blood cells taken down a microscope, in eight classes, shrunk to twenty-eight by twenty-eight colour pixels.

46
00:04:26.825 --> 00:04:36.625
Telling those cell types apart is a real clinical task: a differential count is part of a routine blood test, and automated microscopes already do it.

47
00:04:36.625 --> 00:04:41.500
The best dense network has one hidden layer of a hundred and twenty-eight units.

48
00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:45.575
Three hundred and two thousand two hundred and sixteen weights.

49
00:04:45.575 --> 00:04:50.000
It reaches a validation accuracy of nought point seven nine three.

50
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.175
The small convolutional network has two convolution blocks.

51
00:04:54.175 --> 00:04:57.725
Seventeen thousand six hundred and forty weights.

52
00:04:57.725 --> 00:05:00.800
It reaches nought point eight eight seven.

53
00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.600
Seventeen times fewer weights, and about nine percentage points better.

54
00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:06.425
Why?

55
00:05:06.425 --> 00:05:13.125
For the same reason as the protein network in Block two, and it is worth saying in plain words one more time.

56
00:05:13.125 --> 00:05:22.150
The dense network has to discover from the data that nearby pixels belong together, and that a detector which works in one place should also work in another.

57
00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:27.875
Those are facts about images, and it has to learn them from examples, spending weights on it.

58
00:05:27.875 --> 00:05:33.300
The convolutional network does not have to learn them, because they are built into its shape.

59
00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:37.725
It can spend all its capacity on what the cells actually look like.

60
00:05:37.725 --> 00:05:41.625
This is Session nine's lesson, seen from the other side.

61
00:05:41.625 --> 00:05:48.525
In Session nine you learned that more weights do not help, and that you control capacity with regularisation.

62
00:05:48.525 --> 00:05:56.150
Here you learn the positive version: the right assumptions do help, and they are worth more than a large number of free parameters.

63
00:05:56.750 --> 00:06:02.750
Now a harder and more realistic situation, which is the one you will actually meet in biology.

64
00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:14.900
These are histology tiles: slides of colorectal cancer tissue, stained with haematoxylin and eosin, which is the standard pink-and-purple stain, photographed and cut into small square tiles.

65
00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:20.975
Each tile is labelled with one of nine tissue types, and the slide shows one example of each.

66
00:06:20.975 --> 00:06:30.375
There is tumour epithelium, normal mucosa, cancer-associated stroma, smooth muscle, lymphocytes, debris, adipose tissue, mucus, and background.

67
00:06:30.375 --> 00:06:35.125
Two questions before we go on, and these are worth pausing the video for.

68
00:06:35.125 --> 00:06:41.075
First: if you were a pathologist, what would you actually look at to tell stroma from smooth muscle?

69
00:06:41.075 --> 00:06:44.850
They are both pink and fibrous at low magnification.

70
00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:47.750
Think about what the difference would have to be.

71
00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:57.675
Second, and this is the one that matters methodologically: the notebook trains on tiles from eighty-six slides, and validates on tiles from the same eighty-six slides.

72
00:06:57.675 --> 00:07:00.350
Why might that be too easy a test?

73
00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:10.900
The set-up for the experiment is this: we pretend we only have forty labelled tiles per class for training, and forty for validation, all from those same eighty-six slides.

74
00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:16.475
And then we test on a hundred tiles per class from fifty completely different patients.

75
00:07:16.475 --> 00:07:21.525
Forty per class is a realistic amount of labelled data in biology.

76
00:07:21.525 --> 00:07:26.300
One point two million labelled images, as ImageNet had, is not.

77
00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:31.250
Here are the results, and there are three separate lessons in this table.

78
00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:41.000
Training the small convolutional network from scratch: two hundred and forty-two thousand six hundred and ninety-seven weights, three hundred and sixty training tiles.

79
00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:48.050
Validation accuracy nought point eight six one, test accuracy on the new patients nought point seven four five.

80
00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:52.200
Lesson one: with that little data, the model is unstable.

81
00:07:52.200 --> 00:08:02.350
Running it again with a different random seed, and changing nothing else, gives test accuracies anywhere from nought point seven two one to nought point seven eight six.

82
00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:06.125
The seed alone moves it more than six percentage points.

83
00:08:06.125 --> 00:08:14.250
So if you ever read a paper comparing two methods where the difference is two points and there is one run of each, you now know what to ask.

84
00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:26.150
Lesson two: adding data augmentation, which we come to in a moment, moves the test accuracy to nought point seven nine two, with a range of nought point seven six nine to nought point eight two one.

85
00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:29.675
Better on average, but not in every single seed.

86
00:08:29.675 --> 00:08:33.625
Lesson three, and this is the big one: transfer learning.

87
00:08:33.625 --> 00:08:46.900
Take ResNet-18, a network trained on ImageNet's photographs of everyday objects, freeze it, meaning keep all of its learned weights fixed and refuse to change them, and train only a new final layer on your tiles.

88
00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:49.750
That reaches nought point seven eight.

89
00:08:49.750 --> 00:08:55.900
And take DINOv2, a much larger network, freeze that, and you reach nought point eight three two.

90
00:08:55.900 --> 00:08:59.775
Now look at one more thing, which runs across every row.

91
00:08:59.775 --> 00:09:04.650
Every single model scores clearly higher on validation than on test.

92
00:09:04.650 --> 00:09:10.725
Nought point eight six one against nought point seven four five, and so on down the table.

93
00:09:10.725 --> 00:09:13.000
That is not bad luck.

94
00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:21.600
The validation tiles come from the same slides as the training tiles, which means the same patients, the same staining run, the same scanner.

95
00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:25.375
The model can recognise the slide rather than the tissue.

96
00:09:25.375 --> 00:09:36.300
This is Session eight's homology trap wearing a different hat: there, two proteins related by evolution sat on both sides of the split; here, two tiles from the same patient do.

97
00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:39.850
The rule that comes out of this is simple and general.

98
00:09:39.850 --> 00:09:47.925
When your examples come in groups, proteins in families, tiles in patients, cells in experiments, the honest split is by group.

99
00:09:48.525 --> 00:09:57.725
I want to dwell on the transfer learning result, because it is surprising if you think about it properly, and it is the single most useful technique in this session.

100
00:09:57.725 --> 00:10:01.125
ResNet-18 was trained on photographs.

101
00:10:01.125 --> 00:10:03.875
Cats, cars, mushrooms, boats.

102
00:10:03.875 --> 00:10:07.475
It has never seen a piece of human tissue in its life.

103
00:10:07.475 --> 00:10:11.475
We froze it, so it learned nothing about tissue from us either.

104
00:10:11.475 --> 00:10:17.075
All we trained was one new final layer on top of the features it already produces.

105
00:10:17.075 --> 00:10:23.250
And it matches a convolutional network trained from scratch on the actual tissue with augmentation.

106
00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:27.900
DINOv2 does better still, and it is stranger in two ways.

107
00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:37.550
The first is that DINOv2 is not even a convolutional network: it is a Vision Transformer, the architecture of Session eleven applied to image patches.

108
00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:40.225
The second is how it was trained.

109
00:10:40.225 --> 00:10:50.875
DINOv2 was trained on a hundred and forty-two million images with no labels at all, by a method called self-supervised learning: nobody told it what was in any picture.

110
00:10:50.875 --> 00:10:54.875
And its features are the best of all on tissue it has never seen.

111
00:10:54.875 --> 00:10:56.875
Why does this work?

112
00:10:56.875 --> 00:11:01.475
Because the early and middle layers of a vision network do not learn cats.

113
00:11:01.475 --> 00:11:07.475
They learn edges, textures, gradients, repeated structures, the shapes that boundaries make.

114
00:11:07.475 --> 00:11:12.500
Those are properties of images in general, and a tissue section is an image.

115
00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:19.250
The cat-specific part lives in the last layer or two, which is exactly the part we throw away and replace.

116
00:11:19.250 --> 00:11:23.750
Reusable models like this have a name: foundation models.

117
00:11:23.750 --> 00:11:27.175
And the recipe generalises beyond images.

118
00:11:27.175 --> 00:11:39.150
Session eleven shows you the same thing for proteins, where a model trained on hundreds of millions of sequences, with no labels, produces representations that work for tasks it was never trained on.

119
00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:46.300
The practical advice is simply this: if you have a few hundred labelled examples, do not train a network from scratch.

120
00:11:46.300 --> 00:11:49.775
Start from one that someone else trained on millions.

121
00:11:50.375 --> 00:11:56.725
When a model is wrong, the interesting question is not how often, but which things it confuses with which.

122
00:11:56.725 --> 00:12:01.625
That is what a confusion matrix is for, and it is worth reading properly.

123
00:12:01.625 --> 00:12:09.450
The worst class here is cancer-associated stroma: only nought point five zero of stroma tiles are classified correctly.

124
00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:11.425
Where do the rest go?

125
00:12:11.425 --> 00:12:15.425
Most often to smooth muscle, at nought point three seven.

126
00:12:15.425 --> 00:12:21.850
And the confusion runs the other way too: smooth muscle is called stroma nought point two six of the time.

127
00:12:21.850 --> 00:12:28.300
The second confusion is normal mucosa being called tumour epithelium, at nought point one five.

128
00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:33.850
Now, here is why those two pairs are worth your attention rather than being a defect to shrug at.

129
00:12:33.850 --> 00:12:37.650
Both are genuinely hard for human pathologists too.

130
00:12:37.650 --> 00:12:48.550
Cancer-associated stroma and smooth muscle are both fibrous, pink, elongated tissue, and distinguishing them often needs context beyond a small tile, or extra stains.

131
00:12:48.550 --> 00:12:54.950
Normal mucosa and tumour epithelium sit on a continuum, which is what cancer progression is.

132
00:12:54.950 --> 00:13:01.350
So the model's errors are not random: they are concentrated exactly where the biology is ambiguous.

133
00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:06.700
That is a good sign about the model, and it is also the right way to report results.

134
00:13:06.700 --> 00:13:12.025
An overall accuracy of nought point eight three tells a clinician almost nothing.

135
00:13:12.025 --> 00:13:20.600
Saying that it confuses stroma with smooth muscle half the time, and that this is a known hard distinction, tells them what they can use it for.

136
00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:24.850
Now the thing a convolutional network does not give you.

137
00:13:24.850 --> 00:13:37.825
Data augmentation means making extra training examples by altering the ones you have, shifting, rotating, mirroring, changing the brightness and colour slightly, and telling the network the label has not changed.

138
00:13:37.825 --> 00:13:42.575
In every epoch the network sees a slightly different copy of each image.

139
00:13:42.575 --> 00:13:46.625
People often describe it as a way of making more data.

140
00:13:46.625 --> 00:13:49.400
That is true but it misses the point.

141
00:13:49.400 --> 00:13:55.325
The real description is this: augmentation is how you tell the network what should not matter.

142
00:13:55.325 --> 00:14:01.375
And which transformations should not matter is a statement about the biology, not about the network.

143
00:14:01.375 --> 00:14:04.850
A tissue section on a glass slide has no up.

144
00:14:04.850 --> 00:14:09.325
The pathologist put it on the slide in whatever orientation it landed.

145
00:14:09.325 --> 00:14:16.600
So rotating it by ninety degrees, or mirroring it, produces an image that is just as valid, with the same label.

146
00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:24.650
Small colour shifts imitate the real differences in staining between laboratories and between days, which your model will certainly meet.

147
00:14:24.650 --> 00:14:30.675
So for histology: rotations, flips and colour jitter are all correct augmentations.

148
00:14:30.675 --> 00:14:36.025
Now the counter-example, to show that this is a real decision and not a default.

149
00:14:36.025 --> 00:14:40.800
A handwritten six rotated by a hundred and eighty degrees is a nine.

150
00:14:40.800 --> 00:14:49.600
If you augment a digit classifier with rotations, you are actively teaching it that sixes and nines are the same thing, and you will make it worse.

151
00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:53.725
There is no general answer to which augmentations are right.

152
00:14:53.725 --> 00:14:56.625
You have to know what your data means.

153
00:14:57.225 --> 00:15:02.700
And now we measure it, which is the part I like, because it turns an argument into a number.

154
00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:05.075
The experiment is simple.

155
00:15:05.075 --> 00:15:12.975
Take the nine hundred test tiles, apply a transformation to each one, and count how often the model's predicted class changes.

156
00:15:12.975 --> 00:15:19.450
A truly invariant classifier would change none of them: it would be the same tissue, so the same answer.

157
00:15:19.450 --> 00:15:21.775
Without augmentation.

158
00:15:21.775 --> 00:15:27.275
Shift the tile by eight pixels: the answer changes for five per cent of tiles.

159
00:15:27.275 --> 00:15:32.475
Five per cent, for a shift of eight pixels, is the architecture earning its keep.

160
00:15:32.475 --> 00:15:36.625
That is Block one's equivariance plus pooling, doing its job.

161
00:15:36.625 --> 00:15:42.350
Now rotate by ninety degrees: the answer changes for eighteen per cent of tiles.

162
00:15:42.350 --> 00:15:45.025
Mirror it: seventeen per cent.

163
00:15:45.025 --> 00:15:50.575
Nearly one tile in five gets a different diagnosis because someone turned the slide.

164
00:15:50.575 --> 00:15:53.625
Nothing in convolution handles that.

165
00:15:53.625 --> 00:15:56.225
Nothing in pooling handles it.

166
00:15:56.225 --> 00:15:59.625
The architecture gives you shifts and only shifts.

167
00:15:59.625 --> 00:16:09.200
Train the same network with rotated and mirrored copies, and those numbers roughly halve: the rotation figure falls to nine per cent, the mirror figure to seven.

168
00:16:09.200 --> 00:16:14.325
So the network has learned from data a symmetry that its architecture could not give it.

169
00:16:14.325 --> 00:16:16.925
Not perfectly, but much better.

170
00:16:16.925 --> 00:16:18.650
Two footnotes.

171
00:16:18.650 --> 00:16:29.700
The pretrained networks are not rotation-invariant either, and for a reason worth knowing: their training photographs are almost all upright, because people take photographs the right way up.

172
00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:34.975
So they have learned that up exists, which is true of photographs and false of tissue.

173
00:16:34.975 --> 00:16:47.900
And this is why real pathology pipelines always augment with all four rotations and both flips, and why people went to the trouble of designing rotation-equivariant convolutional networks specifically for pathology.

174
00:16:48.500 --> 00:16:54.050
The same experiment on the blood cells from your lab makes the shape of the problem visible.

175
00:16:54.050 --> 00:17:02.425
Here, instead of just ninety degrees, the cells are rotated by every angle, and we plot accuracy against the angle of rotation.

176
00:17:02.425 --> 00:17:06.050
Without augmentation, the curve sags in the middle.

177
00:17:06.050 --> 00:17:09.450
The worst performance is at forty-five degrees.

178
00:17:09.450 --> 00:17:21.500
That makes sense if you think about what the network has: at ninety degrees, at least the image grid lines up with itself again, but at forty-five nothing does, and the network has never seen anything like it.

179
00:17:21.500 --> 00:17:26.050
With augmentation, the curve is essentially flat across all angles.

180
00:17:26.050 --> 00:17:29.825
The network has learned that orientation does not matter.

181
00:17:29.825 --> 00:17:39.150
And notice one more detail, which is honest and worth pointing out: with augmentation, the accuracy on upright cells is slightly lower than it was without.

182
00:17:39.150 --> 00:17:46.750
You spent some capacity on being orientation-independent, and that capacity is no longer available for the upright case.

183
00:17:46.750 --> 00:17:53.225
That is a trade, and it is almost always the right one here, because real cells arrive at every angle.

184
00:17:53.225 --> 00:17:56.100
But it is a trade, not a free lunch.

185
00:17:56.700 --> 00:18:02.650
Now the last idea of the session, and it is the one that connects this session to the two that follow.

186
00:18:02.650 --> 00:18:06.750
Convolution is not only for photographs and sequences.

187
00:18:06.750 --> 00:18:15.650
Some biological data is an image even though it does not look like one, and the most important example is the contact map of a protein.

188
00:18:15.650 --> 00:18:21.500
Start from what a structure is: a list of coordinates in three dimensions, one per atom.

189
00:18:21.500 --> 00:18:27.350
A contact map throws almost all of that away and keeps one thing: which residues touch.

190
00:18:27.350 --> 00:18:37.500
For a protein of L residues, it is an L by L grid, and the pixel at row i, column j is black if residues i and j are close in space, white if not.

191
00:18:37.500 --> 00:18:50.225
Close needs a definition, and the usual one is that their C-beta atoms, the first carbon of the side chain, are within eight ångström of each other, which is roughly the distance at which two side chains can touch.

192
00:18:50.225 --> 00:18:56.250
Three things follow directly from that definition, and you can see all three on the map on this slide.

193
00:18:56.250 --> 00:18:58.200
It is symmetric.

194
00:18:58.200 --> 00:19:04.850
If i touches j then j touches i, so every contact appears twice, mirrored across the diagonal.

195
00:19:04.850 --> 00:19:12.075
The diagonal is always black, because every residue is close to itself and to its immediate neighbours in the chain.

196
00:19:12.075 --> 00:19:16.025
And the contacts far from the diagonal are the interesting ones.

197
00:19:16.025 --> 00:19:22.725
Those join residues that are far apart along the sequence but brought close together by the way the chain folds.

198
00:19:22.725 --> 00:19:25.475
They are what makes a fold a fold.

199
00:19:25.475 --> 00:19:32.425
Look at the four coloured pairs on the slide, matched between the three-dimensional structure on the left and the map on the right.

200
00:19:32.425 --> 00:19:38.250
This is beta-globin, the beta chain of haemoglobin, a hundred and forty-six residues.

201
00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:49.725
The green pair is within a single helix, so it sits right next to the diagonal: in a helix, residue i touches residue i plus three and i plus four, which are its neighbours on the next turn.

202
00:19:49.725 --> 00:19:53.950
That is why helices show up as a thick band along the diagonal.

203
00:19:53.950 --> 00:20:03.675
The blue, orange and purple pairs are on different helices that are packed against each other, so they sit far from the diagonal, in short stripes where two helices meet.

204
00:20:03.675 --> 00:20:14.025
Beta-globin has a hundred and ninety-seven contacts between residues at least six apart in sequence, of which a hundred and fifty are long-range, at least twenty-four apart.

205
00:20:14.025 --> 00:20:18.075
Now, why am I showing you this in a session about convolution?

206
00:20:18.075 --> 00:20:20.575
Because of the word patterns.

207
00:20:20.575 --> 00:20:22.900
Helices make bands.

208
00:20:22.900 --> 00:20:27.775
Pairs of beta strands make lines, running along or across the diagonal.

209
00:20:27.775 --> 00:20:35.800
Those are local patterns in a two-dimensional grid, which is exactly and precisely what a two-dimensional convolution is good at.

210
00:20:35.800 --> 00:20:45.225
One more property, which connects back to Block one: a contact map does not change when you rotate or move the protein, because it only records distances.

211
00:20:45.225 --> 00:20:52.450
It is invariant to rotation and translation by construction, which is a very convenient thing to hand a network.

212
00:20:53.050 --> 00:20:58.225
And once a protein is an image, you can do to it everything this session has taught.

213
00:20:58.225 --> 00:21:10.275
This graph shows the best contact predictor at each CASP, which is the community experiment where structure-prediction methods are tested blind on structures that have been solved but not yet published.

214
00:21:10.275 --> 00:21:13.250
Look at what happens in twenty sixteen.

215
00:21:13.250 --> 00:21:23.075
That is when deep residual convolutional networks arrived, predicting the whole contact map at once as an image, rather than scoring residue pairs one at a time.

216
00:21:23.075 --> 00:21:29.250
Accuracy went to forty-seven per cent, and by twenty eighteen it had passed seventy per cent.

217
00:21:29.250 --> 00:21:39.125
Notice the word residual: this is ResNet from the start of this block, doing real work in structural biology three years after it won a photograph competition.

218
00:21:39.125 --> 00:21:43.350
That road leads directly to Session twelve and AlphaFold.

219
00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:51.725
The step from predicting contacts to predicting structures, and the step from convolution to attention, are what Session twelve is about.

220
00:21:52.325 --> 00:21:55.575
Two slides to finish, both close to home.

221
00:21:55.575 --> 00:22:01.325
This was a competition, run on Kaggle by Emma Lundberg's group here at SciLifeLab.

222
00:22:01.325 --> 00:22:12.400
The data is from the Human Protein Atlas: microscope images of human cells where a particular protein has been stained, and the task is to say which parts of the cell the protein is in.

223
00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:21.150
Thirty-one thousand and seventy-two training images, eleven thousand seven hundred and two test images, twenty-eight possible locations.

224
00:22:21.150 --> 00:22:30.825
And notice that this is the same biological question as our protein network in Block two, asked of a different kind of data: where in the cell is this protein?

225
00:22:30.825 --> 00:22:33.800
There, from the amino acid sequence.

226
00:22:33.800 --> 00:22:35.825
Here, from a picture.

227
00:22:35.825 --> 00:22:39.950
The difficulties are exactly the ones this session has been about.

228
00:22:39.950 --> 00:22:45.275
The classes are extremely unbalanced: some compartments are common, some are rare.

229
00:22:45.275 --> 00:22:51.425
And an image can have several labels at once, because a protein can genuinely be in two places.

230
00:22:51.425 --> 00:23:01.275
The winning team's F1 score, averaged over the twenty-eight classes, was nought point five nine three, against nought point four seven for the previous best method.

231
00:23:01.275 --> 00:23:04.625
That is a twenty-six per cent improvement.

232
00:23:04.625 --> 00:23:14.800
And then the honest part: expert human annotators still did better, with a precision of nought point seven four and a recall of nought point six nine on similar data.

233
00:23:14.800 --> 00:23:17.325
This is not a solved problem.

234
00:23:17.325 --> 00:23:20.575
It is a problem where the machines became useful.

235
00:23:21.175 --> 00:23:26.475
Finally, where this is going, and it is being built a few kilometres from this lecture.

236
00:23:26.475 --> 00:23:36.775
Alpha Cell is SciLifeLab and KTH's effort to create predictive models that explain how human cells function, how they adapt, and how they change in disease.

237
00:23:36.775 --> 00:23:45.000
It is led by Jan Ellenberg and Mathias Uhlén, with thirty-six and a half million dollars from the Knut and Alice Wallenberg Foundation.

238
00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:59.300
The first phase builds on data that already exists, in the Human Protein Atlas: more than ten million manually annotated bioimages, and single-cell gene expression profiles across eighty-one cell types from thirty-one human tissues.

239
00:23:59.300 --> 00:24:04.900
Which is to say, it starts from the same images as the competition on the previous slide.

240
00:24:04.900 --> 00:24:15.975
Later phases add three-dimensional microscopy at molecular resolution, live-cell time series, an integrated four-dimensional predictive model, and then applications.

241
00:24:15.975 --> 00:24:19.025
I show you this because of where it starts.

242
00:24:19.025 --> 00:24:26.700
It starts from image models of exactly the kind you have met today, and from the step from classifying a tile to modelling a cell.

243
00:24:26.700 --> 00:24:33.050
That step is not obviously possible, and it is being attempted now, with the techniques in this lecture.

244
00:24:33.050 --> 00:24:39.200
As always with recent work: this is an ambition and a research programme, not a finished result.

245
00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:41.650
Treat the claims as claims.

246
00:24:42.250 --> 00:24:44.375
That is Session ten.

247
00:24:44.375 --> 00:24:58.975
To summarise in one sentence, the one from the first slide: a convolutional layer assumes that nearby inputs belong together and reuses the same small detector everywhere, and that assumption is worth more than a hundred thousand extra weights.

248
00:24:58.975 --> 00:25:06.450
And the price of it, which we found in Block two: the network stops knowing where things were, unless you arrange for it to keep that.

249
00:25:06.450 --> 00:25:09.500
Session eleven is where we arrange it.

250
00:25:09.500 --> 00:25:17.800
Models that read a sequence in order and relate distant positions: recurrent networks, LSTMs, attention and the Transformer.

251
00:25:17.800 --> 00:25:30.900
And SignalP, which follows that entire history, from a small feed-forward network in nineteen ninety-five to a protein language model in twenty twenty-two, on exactly the signal peptides our filters found today.

252
00:25:30.900 --> 00:25:39.675
The Session ten page has all of this written out, and its "Check your understanding" section at the end is the fastest way to find out whether it stuck.

253
00:25:39.675 --> 00:25:43.725
The course notebook is linked in the box under the page's title.

254
00:25:43.725 --> 00:25:45.350
See you there.

